VERDICT: PASS -- `auth supplicant-mac` works.  It is a per-MAC override of the port's
         authentication settings, not the pre-authorisation that the case title
         suggests.  Finding out which it is was the first result of the case.
      the bare command pre-authorises ... NO    (the named MAC still got 0 of 4 ARP
                                                 replies and had to authenticate)
      the bare command restricts ........ NO    (a MAC that was never named
                                                 authenticated normally, EAP-Success at
                                                 1.024 s)
      force-authorized admits that MAC .. YES   (3 of 4 ARP replies after sending ZERO
                                                 EAPOL frames; the DUT lists it as Mode
                                                 NONE, ForceAuthorized, no username)
      and only that MAC ................. YES   (the control MAC got 0 of 4 on the same
                                                 port with the same instrument)
      force-unauthorized refuses it ..... YES   (1 EAPOL frame in 40 s and 0 of 4, even
                                                 with valid credentials)
      and only that MAC ................. YES   (the control MAC authenticated at
                                                 1.025 s in the same configuration)
      removal is real in the dataplane .. YES   (the named MAC went back to 0 of 4)
FINDING: the per-MAC entry holds only one setting.  A second
         `auth supplicant-mac <MAC> <parameter>` silently replaces the first.  So the
         per-MAC re-authentication override cannot be used at any period other than the
         3600 second default.  STEP 4 holds the proof.
CAVEAT:  This run did not prove that the setting survives a reboot.  Proving that needs
         `write`, and this run deliberately left startup-config untouched.  The run
         proved instead that the line appears in and disappears from
         `show running-config`.  Four of the per-MAC overrides were not measured by
         behaviour: `quiet-period`, `supp-timeout`, `max-req` and `max-reauth-req`.  The
         `mask` sub-option, which would name a range of MACs, was not used.  The
         single-setting finding in STEP 4 was proved from the configuration, not from a
         timed run: that would need a 3600 second window.
