awplus# show auth supplicant interface port1.0.48 brief
port1.0.48  48   NONE 020d.b900.4277 ForceUnauthorized --              --

  DUT log: 802.1X[1094]: Force Unauthorized for 02-0d-b9-00-42-77 on port1.0.48

  => STEP 3 PASS: `force-unauthorized` refuses the named MAC and only that MAC.  The
     named MAC held valid credentials and was still refused.  The control MAC, in the
     same configuration, authenticated to EAP-Success at 1.025 s.  Because the
     configuration follows the MAC and the result follows the configuration, the effect
     is not an accident of which MAC was chosen.
     The tool printed MISMATCH on S4c, and that is the honest answer.  The leg was
     declared `--expect failure`, meaning an EAP-Failure frame.  The DUT does not send
     one.  It does not even send an EAP-Request/Identity.  The refusal is silence: the
     EAPOL-Start is never answered.  `eapol.py` records neither Success nor Failure as
     a timeout, which is the truthful reading of a wire that carried one frame in 40
     seconds.
     One note on how the legs were run.  The first attempt put both MACs in one
     `eapol.py` run, which is the preferred shape.  Both timed out, including the
     control MAC, which authenticates reliably on its own.  That is
     `auth host-mode single-host` behaving as it should: with two EAPOL-Starts 3
     milliseconds apart on a single-host port, one supplicant takes the port and the
     other gets no reply.  Here the forced-unauthorized entry took the slot.  The legs
     were therefore run one at a time with `dot1x initialize` between them, and that is
     what is graded.  They are still a pair: same configuration, same minute, same
     instrument, 100 seconds apart.

STEP 4 -- the per-MAC entry holds only one setting                           PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: four commands, each followed by its own read.  Nothing else changed.

awplus(config-if)# no auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277
awplus(config-if)# auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauthentication
  running-config:  auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauthentication
  show auth:       MAC Address: 020d.b900.4277
                     portControl: Auto
                     reAuthenticate: enabled          <-- the override works
                     reAuthPeriod: 3600

awplus(config-if)# auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauth-period 15
  running-config:  auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauth-period 15      <-- one line only
  show auth:       MAC Address: 020d.b900.4277
                     portControl: Auto
                     reAuthenticate: disabled         <-- the first setting has gone
                     reAuthPeriod: 15

awplus(config-if)# auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauthentication
  running-config:  auth supplicant-mac 020d.b900.4277 reauthentication
  show auth:         reAuthenticate: enabled
                     reAuthPeriod: 3600               <-- the second setting has gone too

  => STEP 4 PASS, and this is a finding.  No warning was printed, no `% ` line, and no
     log entry was made.  The entry holds exactly one setting.  Each new
     `auth supplicant-mac <MAC> <parameter>` replaces the whole entry instead of adding
     to it, and it does so in both directions.  An earlier attempt with three commands
     left only the last one in the running-config, which is how this was noticed.
     The effect is real, not cosmetic: the per-MAC re-authentication override cannot be
     used at any period other than the 3600 second default, because setting the period
     turns re-authentication off again.  The same applies to every other pair of
     settings.  Here the discarded setting is not even left as a second line in the
     running-config for an operator to notice.
     What this run does not establish: whether one setting per entry is intended.  That
     needs the command reference or a developer.  Either way the operator is not told.

